🎯 Perfect Interview Answer — Full Story
"Before Delta Lake, plain Data Lakes built on S3 or ADLS had serious problems — no ACID transactions causing data corruption, no support for updates or deletes making GDPR compliance impossible, failed jobs leaving dirty data, no schema enforcement allowing bad data in, and no time travel meaning accidental deletes were permanent losses.
To solve all these problems, Databricks introduced Delta Lake in 2016 — an open source storage layer that adds a Transaction Log on top of Parquet files. This transaction log is what enables ACID transactions, time travel, schema enforcement, and DML operations like MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE.
But companies were still maintaining separate Data Lakes and Data Warehouses — same data duplicated in two places, ETL pipelines breaking, different teams seeing different versions of data. So Databricks introduced Lakehouse Architecture in 2020 — combining cheap open storage of Data Lake with reliability of Data Warehouse on ONE platform with ONE copy of data, powered by Delta Lake.
Then in 2023, with the rise of GenAI and LLMs, Lakehouse alone was not enough. So Databricks evolved into the Databricks Intelligence Platform — adding Mosaic AI for end-to-end ML and GenAI, Vector Search for RAG applications, built-in AI Copilot in notebooks, and extending Unity Catalog to govern not just data but also AI models and agents. The platform itself became intelligent."
